{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"All obligations remain disposed with no pending work and audit-unit capacity exhausted, so this step continues the authorized source work on the deferred transport surface by closing the three concurrency questions left open in the previous step and reading the in-process transport's delivery contract that the captured tests rest on. Submission is a feedback-only research product (no map version). Answered: (1) pooled connections are exclusive, because getPooledConn takes connPoolLock, pops the last entry of the per-target slice, nils the slot and truncates before returning (net_transport.go 366-378), and returnConn only re-pools below maxPool (434-446); (2) the NetworkTransport consumer channel is created unbuffered (`consumeCh: make(chan RPC)` in the constructor at 211-231), so each inbound connection's handler blocks on its dispatch send until the Raft main loop receives, which serialises inbound handling and makes head-of-line blocking the normal condition the heartbeat fast path shortcuts; (3) the in-process transport delivers by pushing an RPC with a one-buffered response channel onto the peer's consumer channel under a send timeout and then waiting under a separate response timeout (inmem_transport.go 176-199, from every typed RPC method at 101-174), distinguishing 'send timed out' from 'command timed out', so a send timeout means not delivered while a command timeout can return an error even though the peer accepted and may still process the request. The feedback states plainly that nothing was evaluated for defects. Remaining list keeps the surface deferred and precise: StreamLayer/TCPTransport listener-dialer and TLS wiring plus fatal listener error surfacing, the stream-context machinery (setupStreamContext/getStreamContext/CloseStreams) and whether it affects in-flight RPCs, the in-process peer registration and consumer-channel capacity, whether an address change can race an in-flight dial in getProviderAddressOrFallback, the un-re-checked heartbeat classification against what the leader emits, and the previously recorded open items (installSnapshot reachability, the compacted-leader variant, and execution corroboration of the two reading-based consequences). Preflight validation returns valid with no diagnostics.\"}"}}
